# gor-[[.Name]]

A [gor](https://github.com/karurosux/go-on-rails) generator plugin. Once
installed, it runs as `gor g [[.Name]]` inside any gor app.

## Develop

```bash
gor new testapp && cd testapp                 # a throwaway app to test against
gor plugins install ../gor-[[.Name]] --project --link
gor g [[.Name]]                               # run it
go build ./... && go test ./...               # verify the result compiles
```

`--link` is a dev install: the plugin is symlinked, so edits here apply
immediately — just rerun with `gor g [[.Name]] --force`. (Don't commit the
symlink; teammates install the published plugin instead.)

## How it works

`plugin.json` declares everything; gor interprets it — plugins never
execute code:

- **files** — templates rendered into the app. Template data:
  {{.Module}}, {{.AppName}}, {{.Args}} (extra CLI args). Files ending
  `.go.tmpl` are gofmt-checked. Add `"delims": "square"` to a file that
  contains runtime Go-template syntax, then use square-bracket delimiters
  for plugin values in that file (as this scaffold's templates do).
  A `{{timestamp}}` token in a path (e.g.
  `migrations/{{timestamp}}_create_x.sql`) becomes a fresh goose version
  at run time; re-runs reuse the already-written file instead of minting
  a second migration.
- **injections** — rendered snippets inserted (idempotently) before a
  marker. Markers available in generated apps: `// gor:imports`,
  `// gor:routes` (routes.go); `{/* gor:routes */}`,
  `{/* gor:protected-routes */}`, `{/* gor:nav */}` (frontend);
  `// gor:workers`, `// gor:periodic` (jobs); `# gor:services`
  (docker-compose).
- **env** — appended to `.env.example`/`.env` unless the sentinel key
  already exists.
- **postRun** — allowlisted: `"tidy"` or `"sqlc"`.
- **message** — printed after a successful run.

## Publish

Push this directory as a git repo. Users install it with:

```bash
gor plugins install https://github.com/you/gor-[[.Name]]
```
